WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:02.687
Welcome to this Java tutorial.

00:00:02.687 --> 00:00:05.399
You'll use lambdas, small expressions that

00:00:05.399 --> 00:00:08.305
supply behavior, and method references, which

00:00:08.305 --> 00:00:10.177
refer to existing operations.

00:00:10.177 --> 00:00:12.825
You'll pass those rules to one helper and

00:00:12.825 --> 00:00:15.924
explain when the chosen operation actually runs.

00:00:16.576 --> 00:00:19.567
Applications often keep the same processing step

00:00:19.567 --> 00:00:21.561
while callers choose one detail.

00:00:21.561 --> 00:00:24.241
Passing behavior lets a shared method serve

00:00:24.241 --> 00:00:27.045
those different choices, so the program needs

00:00:27.045 --> 00:00:28.603
fewer copies to maintain.

00:00:28.603 --> 00:00:31.283
We'll use an interface contract to make the

00:00:31.283 --> 00:00:33.651
expected input and result types clear.

00:00:34.303 --> 00:00:37.218
A campus visitor desk needs cleaned names for

00:00:37.218 --> 00:00:39.938
some displays and guest labels for others.

00:00:39.938 --> 00:00:41.168
Each input is text.

00:00:41.168 --> 00:00:44.277
A cleaning rule removes ordinary spaces around a

00:00:44.277 --> 00:00:44.601
name.

00:00:44.601 --> 00:00:47.710
A labeling rule adds Guest, a colon, and a space

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:48.746
before the name.

00:00:48.746 --> 00:00:51.467
The helper must apply exactly the rule its

00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:52.503
caller supplies.

00:00:53.151 --> 00:00:56.003
We'll build one text-rendering helper and use it

00:00:56.003 --> 00:00:56.954
with both rules.

00:00:56.954 --> 00:00:59.330
First we'll trace a worked set of names.

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:02.004
Then you'll predict a different set before we

00:01:02.004 --> 00:01:02.420
run it.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.034
The report should show the chosen formatting

00:01:05.034 --> 00:01:07.351
while the helper's body stays the same.

00:01:08.061 --> 00:01:10.921
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:01:10.921 --> 00:01:13.913
Main.java and define the operation our helper

00:01:13.913 --> 00:01:14.711
can receive.

00:01:18.748 --> 00:01:21.332
Java's Function interface describes one

00:01:21.332 --> 00:01:23.783
operation with an input and a result.

00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:26.963
We'll import it, then give LabelPrinter a render

00:01:26.963 --> 00:01:30.077
method that receives both the text and the rule

00:01:30.077 --> 00:01:30.673
to apply.

00:01:50.642 --> 00:01:53.383
Function is a functional interface: it has one

00:01:53.383 --> 00:01:55.887
required abstract operation, called apply.

00:01:55.887 --> 00:01:58.509
Its first String type argument describes the

00:01:58.509 --> 00:02:00.833
input; the second describes the result.

00:02:00.833 --> 00:02:03.575
Inside render, apply invokes the supplied rule

00:02:03.575 --> 00:02:04.409
with the text.

00:02:04.409 --> 00:02:07.031
Return sends that result back to the caller.

00:02:07.031 --> 00:02:09.892
The helper doesn't choose the formatting itself.

00:02:09.905 --> 00:02:12.746
Let's add the program's entry point around the

00:02:12.746 --> 00:02:13.858
caller statements.

00:02:13.858 --> 00:02:16.638
Main is the class containing this part of our

00:02:16.638 --> 00:02:19.480
code, and its main method is where the program

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:19.912
starts.

00:02:19.912 --> 00:02:22.506
We'll put the rule choices and their calls

00:02:22.506 --> 00:02:23.680
inside that method.

00:02:31.666 --> 00:02:34.257
The first caller needs surrounding spaces

00:02:34.257 --> 00:02:34.763
removed.

00:02:34.763 --> 00:02:37.543
We'll store a cleaning lambda, then create a

00:02:37.543 --> 00:02:40.324
separate labeling lambda that adds the Guest

00:02:40.324 --> 00:02:40.766
prefix.

00:02:55.049 --> 00:02:58.047
The arrow separates each lambda's parameter from

00:02:58.047 --> 00:02:58.609
its body.

00:02:58.609 --> 00:03:01.357
The target Function type tells Java that the

00:03:01.357 --> 00:03:03.418
parameter and result are Strings.

00:03:03.418 --> 00:03:06.416
In this expression form, the body's value is the

00:03:06.416 --> 00:03:07.416
returned result.

00:03:07.416 --> 00:03:10.101
Assigning clean and tag makes the two rules

00:03:10.101 --> 00:03:12.787
available; it doesn't yet process a name or

00:03:12.787 --> 00:03:13.724
print anything.

00:03:13.748 --> 00:03:16.649
Now let's supply the cleaning rule with padded

00:03:16.649 --> 00:03:19.108
Ari text and the labeling rule with Bo.

00:03:19.108 --> 00:03:21.757
Each call passes two arguments to the same

00:03:21.757 --> 00:03:22.198
helper.

00:03:22.198 --> 00:03:24.973
The surrounding print statement displays the

00:03:24.973 --> 00:03:26.423
String that comes back.

00:03:40.571 --> 00:03:43.466
For the Ari call, render receives the padded

00:03:43.466 --> 00:03:45.046
text and the clean rule.

00:03:45.046 --> 00:03:47.810
Applying that rule removes the surrounding

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:48.270
spaces.

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:51.166
For Bo, the selected rule constructs a guest

00:03:51.166 --> 00:03:51.560
label.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:54.587
The helper follows the same steps because both

00:03:54.587 --> 00:03:57.154
rules satisfy the same input and result

00:03:57.154 --> 00:03:57.746
contract.

00:03:57.781 --> 00:04:00.604
String already has the trim method we need.

00:04:00.604 --> 00:04:03.624
Let's supply that existing operation through a

00:04:03.624 --> 00:04:06.578
method reference, then apply it to padded Bea

00:04:06.578 --> 00:04:06.906
text.

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:25.460
The double colon names behavior to use later.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.739
It doesn't call trim at the assignment.

00:04:27.739 --> 00:04:30.486
When namedClean is applied, the supplied String

00:04:30.486 --> 00:04:32.940
becomes the object whose trim method runs.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:35.570
This compatible method reference performs the

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:38.142
same cleaning operation as our first lambda.

00:04:38.142 --> 00:04:40.830
Let's click Run and inspect the worked report.

00:04:47.449 --> 00:04:49.961
The first line is Ari without surrounding

00:04:49.961 --> 00:04:50.390
spaces.

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:53.330
The second is Guest, a colon and space, then Bo.

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:56.271
The last line is Bea without surrounding spaces.

00:04:56.271 --> 00:04:58.966
The caller supplied a rule and input, render

00:04:58.966 --> 00:05:01.662
invoked the rule, and printing displayed the

00:05:01.662 --> 00:05:02.642
returned result.

00:05:02.642 --> 00:05:05.399
The original Strings weren't edited in place.

00:05:05.492 --> 00:05:08.613
Let's keep both rules and the helper exactly as

00:05:08.613 --> 00:05:09.211
they are.

00:05:09.211 --> 00:05:12.199
We'll change only the three supplied names to

00:05:12.199 --> 00:05:13.527
Maya, Luis and Nora.

00:05:13.527 --> 00:05:16.383
Maya and Nora will still have two spaces on

00:05:16.383 --> 00:05:18.242
either side in their inputs.

00:05:44.506 --> 00:05:47.025
Before running, predict all three lines.

00:05:47.025 --> 00:05:49.606
For each call, identify its input and the

00:05:49.606 --> 00:05:50.488
selected rule.

00:05:50.488 --> 00:05:53.510
Which calls remove surrounding spaces, and which

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:55.273
call adds the chosen prefix?

00:05:55.273 --> 00:05:58.233
Pause here and follow the result through render

00:05:58.233 --> 00:05:59.681
to its print statement.

00:06:07.758 --> 00:06:10.849
Let's click Run and compare your prediction with

00:06:10.849 --> 00:06:12.008
the actual report.

00:06:17.439 --> 00:06:20.239
The report shows Maya, then Guest with Luis,

00:06:20.239 --> 00:06:20.876
then Nora.

00:06:20.876 --> 00:06:23.740
Clean processed the first input; tag produced

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:26.477
the second label; namedClean used the third

00:06:26.477 --> 00:06:28.068
input as trim's receiver.

00:06:28.068 --> 00:06:30.041
Only the caller's text changed.

00:06:30.041 --> 00:06:32.650
The same helper still applied each chosen

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:34.814
operation and returned its result.

00:06:34.876 --> 00:06:37.545
You used a functional interface to describe one

00:06:37.545 --> 00:06:40.156
text operation, supplied behavior with lambdas

00:06:40.156 --> 00:06:42.882
and a compatible method reference, and separated

00:06:42.882 --> 00:06:44.755
creating a rule from applying it.

00:06:44.755 --> 00:06:47.424
For a transfer question, imagine the desk needs

00:06:47.424 --> 00:06:48.389
staff labels too.

00:06:48.389 --> 00:06:51.001
What new rule could the caller supply, and why

00:06:51.001 --> 00:06:53.726
could the existing render method still serve it?
